#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1954|month=December|author=Olive Knox|title=The Evening Star|editor=H{{quote-gloss|arold}} S{{quote-gloss|tanley}} Fry|journal=The Country Guide|location=Winnipeg, Man.|publisher=The Public Press Limited|page=33|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/N027694/page/33/mode/1up|column=1|issn=1485-5577|oclc=1086674526|passage=Lesia put a bowl of '''kutya''', its boiled wheat grains mixed with honey and ground poppy seed, in the center of the table. Papa dipped a wooden spoon into the '''kutya''' and tossed it into the air.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1989|month=October|author=Linda Madl|chapter=Your Army and Mine|title=Sweet Ransom|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Pocket Books|page=232|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sweetransom0000madl/page/232/mode/1up|isbn=0-671-66943-5|passage=The next day, Christmas Day, Elise tried to have as little to do with Nikholai as possible. The day was celebrated with the traditional feast that ended with a pot of '''kutya''', rice boiled with raisins and nuts.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Alisa White Karwowski|chapter=The ‘Holy Supper’|title=A Guide to Russian Adoption: Professional Counseling and Personal Insights|location=Westport, Conn.|publisher=[[w:Greenwood Publishing Group|Praeger]]|section=chapter 8 (Personal Challenges and Joys: John Sergei and Charlie Vitalij)|page=150|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/guidetorussianad0000karw/page/150/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-313-35052-8|passage=The '''kutya''' is eaten from a common dish to symbolize unity. Some families used to throw a spoonful of '''kutya''' up to the ceiling. According to tradition, if the '''kutya''' stuck, there would be a plentiful honey harvest.}}

